Alright, I've just updated the JavaScript to set `--draw-end` and adjusted the sweep delay, and I also added the new `ai-drop-logo-202606` wrapper class with the settle animation separated out. The reduced‑motion handling was previously only targeting the SVG inside `.ai-drop-logo-202606`, like this:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce){
  .ai-drop-logo-202606 > svg{animation:none;}
}
```

But now that the settle animation has been moved out of the SVG's `animation` property and onto the wrapper element (`.ai-drop-logo-202606`), users who prefer reduced motion will still see the settle pop because the wrapper still has an animation applied. The media query needs to suppress animations on both the wrapper and its child SVG.

I could add another rule for the wrapper, but the cleanest approach is to extend the existing selector to include the wrapper itself. That way, any animation on either the container or the SVG will be disabled when reduced‑motion is requested. The CSS should become:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce){
  .ai-drop-logo-202606,
  .ai-drop-logo-202606 > svg{animation:none;}
}
```

This change is minimal, doesn't affect other styles, and directly addresses the issue I just introduced with the separate settle animation. I'll make this edit using the Edit tool, replacing the old media query block with the new one.
